# IAX-9 — a live 16-bit mini-frame time-stamp wrap, inbound, with no full
# frame at the boundary (RFC 5456 §8.1.2, §6.10).
#
# Provenance (LP-1, `Tests/FIXTURES.md` rule 2): `wrap.pcap`, a 77-second
# capture of the maintainer's own traffic against the maintainer's own ASL3
# node, taken 2026-08-09 for exactly this purpose — a session held open past
# 65.536 seconds so that both directions would cross the boundary.
#
# **This is the fixture the expander exists for.** The node's mini time-stamps
# run 65500 → 65520 → 4, and it sends **no full frame at the wrap**. Nothing on
# the wire says an epoch ended. `IAX2MiniTimestampExpander` has to infer it
# from the low 16 bits alone, and in the live session it did: not one frame was
# rejected across the boundary, and the audio did not glitch.
#
# §6.10 says a peer MUST send a full frame at every 0x8000 boundary. This node
# does not. That is worth knowing about real nodes, and it is why the receiving
# side cannot be built to depend on it.
#
# One frame here is *not* a mini frame and must not be treated as an anchor:
# index 6537, the node's ACK of our own re-anchoring Voice frame. §6.9.1 makes
# an ACK "return the same time-stamp it received", so its 65541 is **our**
# clock echoed back, not the node's. It reads as plausible only because both
# clocks started at call setup. A receiver that resynchronised on any full
# frame rather than on media frames alone would adopt the wrong clock here.
#
# The window does not start at the beginning of the call, so it carries no
# origin of its own: by the time it opens, the expander's reference had climbed
# continuously to 65,260. The test seeds it there and that is the only piece of
# session state the fixture does not supply.
#
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py wrap.pcap --stream 0 --dir in --range 6500:6545
# Capture SHA-256: 2658320d536ff332990c0d1d33571e0f7504366089476bddde8bec11772a3053
#
# Each datagram is the UDP payload verbatim — the octets RFC 5456 §5
# defines as an IAX2 frame. `[n]` is the datagram's index in the capture
# stream, so a gap in the indices is a datagram deliberately left out.

# [6500] 65.260s <== mini  call 14253  ts 65280  160B audio
37ad ff00 
